name: trees and recursion
intro: A tree node holds a value and links to children. In a binary search
intro: tree everything in the left subtree is smaller than the node and
intro: everything in the right is larger, so a lookup throws away a whole
intro: side at each step:
intro:
intro:     struct tnode {
intro:         long val;
intro:         struct tnode *left, *right;
intro:     };
intro:
intro: Insert with the link trick from lists, in one short recursion:
intro:
intro:     static int insert(struct tnode **link, long val)
intro:     {
intro:         if (*link == NULL) { /* make a node, *link = it */ }
intro:         if (val < (*link)->val) return insert(&(*link)->left, val);
intro:         if (val > (*link)->val) return insert(&(*link)->right, val);
intro:         return 0;      /* equal: already there */
intro:     }
intro:
intro: A recursive function needs a base case that stops and a step that
intro: makes the problem smaller. A tree is a node plus two smaller trees,
intro: so walks fit: left, node, right visits in ASCENDING order
intro: (in-order); node first is pre-order; node last is post-order, and
intro: post-order is how you free a tree: children before the node, since
intro: after `free(t)` its links are gone. Height is the number of nodes on
intro: the longest path down: 0 when empty, else 1 + the taller child.
intro:
intro: Each call uses stack, and the stack is a few megabytes. Recursion
intro: as deep as the tree is fine for a thousand nodes and a crash for a
intro: million. Worse, YOU do not control the depth: insert 1, 2, 3, 4 in
intro: order and each goes right of the last, a linked list with extra
intro: steps. Balanced, n items cost about log2(n) per lookup; lined up,
intro: n. Sorted input is exactly what real data tends to look like.
intro:
intro: Any recursion can be an explicit stack: an array of node pointers
intro: you push and pop yourself, grown with `realloc`, bounded by memory
intro: and not by the call stack. To walk in-order: push nodes while going
intro: left; pop one and visit it; then go right and repeat. Pre-order:
intro: pop one, visit it, push its right child, then its left.
---
q: bstsort.c -- sort and dedupe with a tree
spec: Reads whole numbers from standard input, one per line, inserts each
spec: into a binary search tree (recursively, taking `struct tnode **`),
spec: and prints every DISTINCT number once, ascending, one per line, by an
spec: in-order walk. A number already in the tree is not inserted again.
spec: Values span the full `long` range. Free the tree (children before
spec: the node) before you exit.
spec:
spec: At most 5000 numbers: reading a 5001st prints `bstsort: too many
spec: numbers` to standard error. A bad line (blank included) prints
spec: `bstsort: line L: bad number`. Both print nothing to standard
spec: output, free the tree, and exit 1. Empty input prints nothing.
file: bstsort.c
prog: #include <errno.h>
prog: #include <stdio.h>
prog: #include <stdlib.h>
prog: 
prog: #define MAXN 5000
prog: 
prog: struct tnode {
prog: 	long val;
prog: 	struct tnode *left, *right;
prog: };
prog: 
prog: /* put val where it belongs; a value already present is left alone */
prog: static int insert(struct tnode **link, long val)
prog: {
prog: 	if (*link == NULL) {
prog: 		struct tnode *n = malloc(sizeof *n);
prog: 		if (n == NULL)
prog: 			return -1;
prog: 		*n = (struct tnode){ .val = val };
prog: 		*link = n;
prog: 		return 0;
prog: 	}
prog: 	if (val < (*link)->val)
prog: 		return insert(&(*link)->left, val);
prog: 	if (val > (*link)->val)
prog: 		return insert(&(*link)->right, val);
prog: 	return 0;
prog: }
prog: 
prog: static void walk(const struct tnode *t)
prog: {
prog: 	if (t == NULL)
prog: 		return;
prog: 	walk(t->left);
prog: 	printf("%ld\n", t->val);
prog: 	walk(t->right);
prog: }
prog: 
prog: /* children before the node: after free(t) its links are gone */
prog: static void free_tree(struct tnode *t)
prog: {
prog: 	if (t == NULL)
prog: 		return;
prog: 	free_tree(t->left);
prog: 	free_tree(t->right);
prog: 	free(t);
prog: }
prog: 
prog: int main(void)
prog: {
prog: 	struct tnode *root = NULL;
prog: 	int status = 0;
prog: 	char line[64];
prog: 	int lineno = 0;
prog: 	while (fgets(line, sizeof line, stdin) != NULL) {
prog: 		lineno++;
prog: 		char *end;
prog: 		errno = 0;
prog: 		long x = strtol(line, &end, 10);
prog: 		if (errno != 0 || end == line || (*end != '\n' && *end != '\0')) {
prog: 			fprintf(stderr, "bstsort: line %d: bad number\n", lineno);
prog: 			status = 1;
prog: 			break;
prog: 		}
prog: 		if (lineno > MAXN) {
prog: 			fprintf(stderr, "bstsort: too many numbers\n");
prog: 			status = 1;
prog: 			break;
prog: 		}
prog: 		if (insert(&root, x) != 0) {
prog: 			fprintf(stderr, "bstsort: out of memory\n");
prog: 			status = 1;
prog: 			break;
prog: 		}
prog: 	}
prog: 	if (status == 0)
prog: 		walk(root);
prog: 	free_tree(root);
prog: 	return status;
prog: }
case: printf '5\n3\n8\n3\n1\n' | bstsort
case: printf '0\n9223372036854775807\n-9223372036854775808\n0\n' | bstsort
case: seq 5000 -1 1 | bstsort | tail -n 2
case: seq 1 5001 | bstsort 2>&1; echo $?
case: bstsort </dev/null; echo $?; printf '1\nx\n' | bstsort 2>&1; echo $?
hint: insert goes left when smaller and right when larger and does nothing when equal; print left subtree, node, right subtree
